+# JSRF v34 — DirectSound GP descriptor mailbox correction
+
+## What the v33 Windows run proved
+
+v33 successfully enabled the generic asynchronous DSP acknowledgement worker, but the configured JSRF mailbox was still wrong.
+
+The runtime printed only the configuration line for `0x8037B810`; there was no later `acknowledged (async)` message. Execution again reached the late DirectSound GP setup, including `GPSMAXSGE = 0xCE`, then the main retail path stopped making visible progress while timer/vblank and worker kernel traffic continued.
+
+The old address came from the first allocation in DirectSound's 16-entry contiguous-memory descriptor table:
+
+- descriptor 0 allocation: `0x8037B000`
+- guessed mailbox: `0x8037B000 + 0x810 = 0x8037B810`
+
+That assumption was the mistake.
+
+## Retail selector proves descriptor 12
+
+The retail command object is created at `0x001A1712` with argument `1`:
+
+```asm
+001A1712  push 1
+001A1714  mov  ecx,eax
+001A1716  call 001A52AF
+```
+
+`sub_001A52AF` stores that argument at object `+8`:
+
+```asm
+001A52AF  mov eax,[esp+4]
+001A52B7  mov [ecx+8],eax
+```
+
+Then `sub_001A52F7` selects the descriptor from the 16-entry table:
+
+```asm
+001A5306  cmp   dword ptr [esi+8],1
+001A530B  setne al
+001A5312  lea   eax,[eax+eax+0xC]
+001A5316  shl   eax,4
+001A531B  lea   eax,[eax+0x001BA798]
+001A5321  mov   [esi+0x10],eax
+```
+
+For object type `1`, `setne al` yields zero, so the descriptor offset is:
+
+`(0 + 0 + 0xC) << 4 = 0xC0`
+
+Therefore the command object uses descriptor index 12, not descriptor index 0.
+
+## Windows allocation evidence
+
+The v33 runtime shows the allocations made from the same `0x001A0E55` call site in descriptor order. Entry 12 is the 0xC000-byte allocation:
+
+- index 0: `0x8037B000`, size `0x2000`
+- ...
+- index 11: `0x803BC000`, size `0x1000`
+- **index 12: `0x803C0000`, size `0xC000`**
+- index 13: `0x803CC000`, size `0x1060`
+- index 14: `0x803D0000`, size `0x13000`
+- index 15: `0x803E4000`, size `0x98`
+
+The retail command sender reaches the descriptor through object `+8 -> +0x10 -> [descriptor]`, then publishes command `3` at base `+0x810` and waits for the DSP to clear it:
+
+```asm
+001A1774  mov eax,[edi+8]
+001A1777  mov eax,[eax+0x10]
+001A177A  mov ebx,[eax]
+...
+001A18C6  add ebx,0x810
+001A18CE  mov [ebx],eax      ; eax = 3
+001A18D0  cmp dword ptr [ebx],0
+001A18D3  jne 001A18D0
+```
+
+So the actual GP command mailbox for this run is:
+
+`0x803C0000 + 0x810 = 0x803C0810`
+
+## v34 change
+
+The Windows runner now configures:
+
+`RECOMP_APU_DSP_ACK=0x803C0810`
+
+instead of `0x8037B810`.
+
+The v33 asynchronous worker is otherwise unchanged. This is intentionally one correction: point the already-existing DSP handshake model at the descriptor the retail object actually selects.
+
+No JSRF gameplay, D3D, audio command generation, allocator behavior, or retail wait loop is replaced.
